P(Event) = \frac{Number of a favorable outcome to an event E}{Total number of a possible outcomes} 
in symbols, P(E) = \frac{n(E)}{n(S)} 
The probability of the event of getting doubles when 2 dice are rolled is; 
P(doubles) = \frac{6}{36} = \frac{1}{6} 
Recall that the probability that an event will happen is between 0 and 1 inclusive. 
\quad A probability of 0 means that an event is impossible to happen. 
\quad A probability of 1 means that an event is certain to happen. 
\quad The closer a probability to 1, is the more likely to happen.

Examples: 
1. A blue and a green dice are rolled. Find the probability of the following events. 
A = {the green die shows 6} 
B = {the sum of the two numbers is 8} 
C = {the sum of the two numbers is less than 6}

